"(PDF) The gene expression profile of extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/7820582_The_gene_expression_profile_of_extraskeletal_myxoid_chondrosarcoma)\nciteturn61view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn51view0\",\"lineno\":225}); Total lines: 2628\nL118: Pathology, L-235, Stanford\nL119: \nL120: University Medical Center, 300\nL121: \nL122: Pasteur Drive, Stanford, CA\nL123: \nL124: 94035, USA.\nL125: \nL126: E-mail: mrijn@stanford.edu\nL127: \nL128: Received: 6 January 2005\nL129: \nL130: Revised: 4 March 2005\nL131: \nL132: Accepted: 14 March 2005\nL133: \nL134: Abstract\nL135: \nL136: Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma (EMC) is a soft tissue tumour that occurs primarily\nL137: \nL138: in the extremities and is characterized by a bal anced translocation most commonly involving\nL139: \nL141: on histology alone can be difﬁcult. Currently, no systemic therapy exists that improves\nL142: \nL143: survival in patients with EMC. In the prese nt study, gene expression proﬁling has been\nL144: \nL145: performed to discover new diagnostic markers and potential therapeutic targets for this\nL146: \nL147: tumour type. Global gene expression proﬁling of ten EMCs and 26 other sarcomas using\nL148: \nL149: 42 000 spot cDNA microarrays revealed that the cases of EMC were closely related to each\nL150: other and distinct from the other tumours pro ﬁled. Signiﬁcance analysis of microarrays\nL151: \nL152: (SAM) identiﬁed 86 genes that distinguished EMC from the other sarcomas with 0.25%\nL153: \nL154: likelihood of false signiﬁcance. NMB, DKK1, DNER, CLCN3,and DEF6 were the top ﬁve\nL155: \nL156: genes in this analysis. In situ hybridization for NMB gene expression on tissue microarrays\nL157: \nL158: (TMAs) containing a total of 1164 specimens representing 62 different sarcoma types and\nL159: 15 different carcinoma types showed that NMB washighlyexpressedin17of22EMCcases\nL160: \nL161: and very rarely expressed in other tumours and thus could function as a novel diagnostic\nL162: \nL163: marker. High levels of expression of PPARG and the gene encoding its interacting protein,\nL164: \nL165: PPARGC1A, in most EMCs suggest activation of lip id metabolism pathways in this tumour.\nL166: \nL167: Small molecule inhibitors for PPARG exist a nd PPARG could be a potential therapeutic\nL168: \nL169: target for EMC.\nL170: Copyright 2005 Pathological Society of Great Britain and Ireland. Published by John\nL171: \nL172: Wiley & Sons, Ltd.\nL173: \nL174: Keywords: extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma; expressing proﬁle; lipid metabolism;\nL175: \nL176: Neuromedin B gene; tissue microarray\nL177: \nL178: Introduction\nL179: \nL180: Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma (EMC) is a\nL181: \nL182: malignant soft tissue tumour that occurs primarily in\nL183: \nL184: the extremities [1]. EMC is most frequently diag-\nL185: \nL186: nosed in middle-aged and elderly men, but has also\nL187: \nL188: been reported in children [2,3]. EMC is character-\nL189: ized by a balanced translocation t(9;22) (q22;q12).\nL190: \nL191: The most common breakpoint involves the EWS gene\nL192: \nL193: on chromosome 22q12 and the TEC gene on chro-\nL194: \nL195: mosome 9q22 [4–6]. The EWS–TEC fusion pro-\nL196: \nL197: tein is a potential transcriptional activator [7]. Less\nL198: \nL199: common translocations result in the fusion of TEC\nL200: \nL201: and other partners [8–10]. The histogenesis of EMC\nL202: \nL203: remains unclear, although studies have suggested pos-\nL204: \nL205: sible neural-neuroendocrine differentiation [11–15]. In\nL206: most cases, EMC is diagnosed based on histological\nL207: \nL208: examination. Distinction of this tumour from other\nL209: \nL210: neoplasms in the differential diagnosis can be difﬁcult.\nL211: \nL212: In this study, we have analysed the global gene expres-\nL213: \nL214: sion proﬁles for ten cases of EMC using cDNA\nL215: \nL216: microarrays. Three new potential markers identiﬁed by\nL217: \nL218: this approach were then validated by in situ hybridiza-\nL219: \nL220: tion on tissue microarrays (TMAs) containing a total\nL221: \nL222: of 1164 cases representing 62 different sarcoma and\nL223: 15 different carcinoma types.\nL224: \nL225: Materials and methods\nL226: \nL227: Tumour samples\nL228: \nL229: Ten cases of EMC were used for the gene expression\nL230: \nL231: studies. The clinical features of these ten cases are\nL232: \nL233: Copyright 2005 Pathological Society of Great Britain and Ireland. Published by John Wiley & Sons, Ltd.\nL234: \nL235: 434 S Subramanian et al\nL236: \nL237: Table 1. Clinical data for the ten cases of EMC tumours used\nL238: \nL239: in the gene expression study\nL240: \nL241: STT\nL242: \nL243: No Diagnosis\nL244: \nL245: Age\nL246: \nL247: (years) Sex Site\nL248: \nL249: Size\nL250: \nL251: (cm)\nL252: \nL253: 2528 EMC 51 Male Perineum NA\nL254: 1169 EMC 66 Male Leg, left 15\nL255: \nL256: 3783 EMC 54 Female Pelvic region, right 9\nL257: \nL258: 3782 EMC 40 Female Thigh, left 7.5\nL259: \nL260: 3780 EMC 61 Female Thigh, right 14\nL261: \nL262: 3699 EMC 53 Male Thigh, right 16\nL263: \nL264: 2003 EMC 69 Male Thigh, right 8\nL265: \nL266: 3696 EMC 49 Male Groin 12\nL267: \nL268: 3697 EMC 69 Male Thigh, right 8\nL269: \nL270: 3698 EMC 71 Male Leg, right 6\nL271: \nL272: STT =soft tissue tumour; EMC =extraskeletal myxoid chondrosar-\nL273: \nL274: coma; NA =not available.\nL275: \nL276: shown in Table 1. All cases examined had classical\nL277: \nL278: histology consistent with EMC and were reviewed\nL279: by at least two pathologists with expertise in soft\nL280: \nL281: tissue tumours. The tissues were frozen and stored at\nL282: \nL283: −80 ◦C at the time of procurement. The institutional\nL284: \nL285: review board at Stanford University approved the\nL286: \nL287: study. For comparison, we used ﬁve cases each of\nL288: \nL289: gastrointestinal stromal tumour (GIST) and synovial\nL290: \nL291: sarcoma (SS); four cases each of leiomyosarcoma\nL292: \nL293: (LMS) and malignant ﬁbrous histiocytoma (MFH);\nL294: \nL295: and eight cases of dermatoﬁbrosarcoma protuberans\nL296: (DFSP). The sarcomas used for comparison purposes\nL297: \nL298: have been previously published [16–18].\nL299: \nL300: Gene expression using cDNA microarrays\nL301: \nL302: The cDNA microarrays used in the study con-\nL303: \nL304: tained a total of 42 000 cDNA spots representing\nL305: \nL306: approximately 28 000 genes or expressed sequence\nL307: \nL308: tags (ESTs) printed on polylysine-coated glass\nL309: \nL310: slides by the Stanford Functional Genomics Facility\nL311: \nL312: (http://www.microarray.org). Preparation and details\nL313: \nL314: of microarray construction, isolation of mRNA from\nL315: tumour tissues, labelling, and hybridization have been\nL316: \nL317: described previously [17]. Brieﬂy, tissue was homog-\nL318: \nL319: enized in Trizol reagent (Invitrogen, Carlsbad, CA,\nL320: \nL321: USA) and total RNA was extracted, followed by\nL322: \nL323: mRNA isolation using the FastTrack 2.0 method\nL324: \nL325: according to the manufacturer’s protocol. Preparation\nL326: \nL327: of Cy3-dUTP (green ﬂuorescent)-labelled cDNA from\nL328: \nL329: reference mRNA and Cy5-dUTP (red ﬂuorescent)-\nL330: \nL331: labelled cDNA from 2 µ g of each tumour specimen\nL332: \nL333: mRNA, microarray hybridization, and washing of\nL334: arrays were performed as previously described [19].\nL335: \nL336: The reference mRNA was obtained from Stratagene\nL337: \nL338: (La Jolla, CA, USA). Microarrays were scanned on\nL339: \nL340: a GenePix 4000 microarray scanner (Axon Instru-\nL341: \nL342: ments, Foster City, CA, USA) and ﬂuorescence ratios\nL343: \nL344: (tumour/reference) were calculated using GenePix\nL345: \nL346: software. The raw data and the image ﬁles from these\nL347: \nL348: experiments are available from the Stanford Microar-\nL349: \nL350: ray Database (http://smd.stanford.edu/) and the ﬁltered\nL351: dataset is available through the accompanying web-\nL352: \nL353: site http://tma.stanford.edu/tma portal/emc/. Data were\nL354: \nL355: selected using the following criteria: control and empty\nL356: \nL357: spots on the arrays were not included in the analysis,\nL358: \nL359: as well as those spots manually ﬂagged as ‘not mea-\nL360: \nL361: surable’. Only cDNA spots with a ratio of signal over\nL362: \nL363: background of at least 2.0 in either the Cy3 or the\nL364: \nL365: Cy5 channel were included. Genes with less than 80%\nL366: \nL367: well-measured data were not selected. A ﬁnal ﬁltering\nL368: criterion was for genes whose expression level differed\nL369: \nL370: by at least four-fold in at least three arrays. Using these\nL371: \nL372: criteria, 2918 genes passed the ﬁltering criteria and\nL373: \nL374: were used for further analysis. Unsupervised hierarchi-\nL375: \nL376: cal clustering analysis [20] and signiﬁcance analysis\nL377: \nL378: of microarrays (SAM) [21] were then performed as\nL379: \nL380: described previously [16].\nL381: \nL382: Tissue microarray (TMA) construction\nL383: \nL384: A TMA of 464 soft tissue tumours (TA-38, TA-\nL385: \nL386: 39) was constructed using a manual tissue arrayer\nL387: (Beecher Instruments, Silver Spring, MD, USA) fol-\nL388: \nL389: lowing previously described techniques [22]. Dupli-\nL390: \nL391: cate 600 µ m cores were taken from parafﬁn-embedded\nL392: \nL393: soft tissue tumour samples archived at the Stanford\nL394: \nL395: University Medical Center, Department of Pathology\nL396: \nL397: between 1995 and 2001. The cores were taken from\nL398: \nL399: areas in the parafﬁn block that were representative of\nL400: \nL401: the diagnosis. Fifty-four different soft tissue tumour\nL402: \nL403: diagnostic entities were represented on TA-38 and TA-\nL404: 39. These tissue arrays are identical to TA-34 and\nL405: \nL406: TA-35 which are described in detail in a previous study\nL407: \nL408: [23]. Furthermore, two new TMAs were generated\nL409: \nL410: that contained 19 cases of EMC, 24 cases of myxoid\nL411: \nL412: liposarcomas, and 25 cases of other sarcomas (TA-\nL413: \nL414: 109) and TA-140 that contained 19 cases of pleomor-\nL415: \nL416: phic adenomas (Supplementary Table 1). A total of\nL417: \nL418: 57 different sarcoma types were represented on TMAs\nL419: \nL420: TA-38/-39, TA-109, and TA-140. We also used a TMA\nL421: (TA-03/008) [24] that contained a total of 121 cases in\nL422: \nL423: duplicate, including 62 chondrosarcomas, ﬁve EMCs,\nL424: \nL425: four each of chondromyxoid ﬁbromas and chondrob-\nL426: \nL427: lastomas, and 30 enchondromas, ten osteosarcomas,\nL428: \nL429: and six osteochondromas. This array added an addi-\nL430: \nL431: tional ﬁve soft tissue tumour (STT) types to the 57\nL432: \nL433: represented on TA-38/TA-39 and TA-109/TA-140, for\nL434: \nL435: a total of 62 STT diagnostic entities. In addition, we\nL436: \nL437: used carcinoma TMAs (TA-41 and TA-42) containing\nL438: 526 cases representing 15 different carcinomas, includ-\nL439: \nL440: ing colon, lung, prostrate, ovary, etc (Supplementary\nL441: \nL442: Table 2). The institutional review board at Stanford\nL443: \nL444: University approved the construction of these TMAs.\nL445: \nL446: In situ hybridization (ISH)\nL447: \nL448: ISH of TMA sections was performed as previously\nL449: \nL450: described [18,22,25,26]. Brieﬂy, sense and anti-sense\nL451: \nL452: RNA probes were generated for NMB, PHLDA1,\nL453: \nL454: LRP5, and KIT by polymerase chain reaction ampli-\nL455: \nL456: ﬁcation with the T7 promoter sequence added to the\nL457: 5end of either forward or reverse primer to gener-\nL458: \nL459: ate sense or anti-sense probes. The primer sequences\nL460: \nL461: J Pathol 2005; 206: 433 –444\nL462: \nL463: Gene expression in EMC 435\nL464: \nL465: Table 2. The ﬁrst 50 genes with the highest differential expressi on in EMC according to SAM analysis. Input parameters for SAM\nL466: \nL467: analysis: imputation engine is 10 nearest neighbour imputer; dat a type is two class-unpaired data; data are in log scale; number of\nL468: permutations is 100; RNG seed is 1234 567; delta fold-changes are 1.86835; and median number of false signiﬁcant is 0.2205\nL469: \nL470: Gene\nL471: \nL472: symbol Description Unigene ID\nL473: \nL474: Score\nL475: \nL476: (d) Fold-change\nL477: \nL478: NMB Neuromedin B Hs.386470 6.560 20.28606\nL479: \nL480: DKK1 Dickkopf homologue 1 (Xenopus laevis) Hs.40499 6.165 65.64312\nL481: \nL482: DNER Delta-notch-like EGF repeat-contai ning transmembrane Hs.234074 5.016 14.68225\nL483: \nL484: CLCN3 Chloride channel 3 Hs.372528 5.010 6.79669\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The gene expression profile of extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/7820582_The_gene_expression_profile_of_extraskeletal_myxoid_chondrosarcoma)\nciteturn61view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn51view0\",\"lineno\":300}); Total lines: 2628\nL170: Copyright 2005 Pathological Society of Great Britain and Ireland. Published by John\nL171: \nL172: Wiley & Sons, Ltd.\nL173: \nL174: Keywords: extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma; expressing proﬁle; lipid metabolism;\nL175: \nL176: Neuromedin B gene; tissue microarray\nL177: \nL178: Introduction\nL179: \nL180: Extraskeletal myxoid chondrosarcoma (EMC) is a\nL181: \nL182: malignant soft tissue tumour that occurs primarily in\nL183: \nL184: the extremities [1]. EMC is most frequently diag-\nL185: \nL186: nosed in middle-aged and elderly men, but has also\nL187: \nL188: been reported in children [2,3]. EMC is character-\nL189: ized by a balanced translocation t(9;22) (q22;q12).\nL190: \nL191: The most common breakpoint involves the EWS gene\nL192: \nL193: on chromosome 22q12 and the TEC gene on chro-\nL194: \nL195: 